{"uid":"cli/retry_support.py","name":"retry_support.py","qualname":"cli/retry_support.py","kind":"module","file":"cli/retry_support.py","line":1,"end_line":282,"language":"py","signature":"","params":[],"returns":"","decorators":[],"bases":[],"modifiers":[],"visibility":"public","doc":{"summary":"Retry support - resume logic shared by the ``retry`` CLI surface.","description":"The CommandSet (``cli.commands.retry_ops``) stays thin: argument parsing,\ncheckpoint selection, variable overrides, elevated-sudo arming and the\nactual relaunch live here, mirroring the ``apt_chain`` support split.\n\nRelaunch semantics:\n\n- chain: :func:`modules.retry_session.newest_failed_checkpoint` picks\n  the freshest aborted/failed checkpoint (chain-name or run-id selector),\n  the recorded variables rehydrate as tokens and every ``--var\n  key=value`` wins over them. The ChainRunner keeps the same run id, so\n  the resumed attempt is auditable against the stored checkpoint.\n- loop: the last non-complete task (TaskStore) or an explicit task id\n  re-enters :func:`modules.lazy_runner.run_loop` with the ordinary\n  ``auto_loop`` flags, so the operator can vary rounds, target, budget.\n- elevation: ``--sudo`` arms :mod:`modules.retry_session` after a\n  warned confirmation; command lines flow through\n  :func:`modules.retry_session.apply_sudo_askpass` so bare ``sudo``\n  invocations switch to ``sudo -A`` without touching operators' code.","params":[],"returns":null,"yields":null,"raises":[],"attributes":[],"examples":[],"notes":[],"see_also":[],"deprecated":"","since":"","style":"plain"},"parent":"","members":["cli/retry_support.py::RUN_ID_PARTS_MIN","cli/retry_support.py::looks_like_run_id","cli/retry_support.py::LastTask","cli/retry_support.py::last_loop_task","cli/retry_support.py::status_snapshot","cli/retry_support.py::consent_to_elevate","cli/retry_support.py::RetryChainReport","cli/retry_support.py::retry_chain"],"inferred_raises":[],"returns_value":null,"value":"","base_uids":[],"subclasses":[],"calls":[],"called_by":[]}
